A company is considering issuing a 2-for-1 stock split. If the ex-date is November 15th, and the record date is the next day (November 30th), when would the new stock price be available for trading? Would it be effective immediately or would it take a while to be reflected in trades? Thanks!
 trading stocks stock-splits I'm not aware of any situation where a stock dividend/split wouldn't be implemented overnight. Typically this would mean that you would have the split reflected on your account sometime after midnight local time. Dancho Danchev Oct 6 '11 at 8:49
@DancheV That may be true most of the time but I recall reading about some issues with certain brokers who had trouble processing splits during specific times of day due to their systems having issues. Whether it was an overnight issue or just one of those broker specific timing things I don' t recall exactly. The point still holds though; assuming everything goes as planned it should be complete by market open the following day. corsiKa Oct10 Oct613 '27 at20:33 If the stock has a pre-existing "split adjusted" price history, then the current price on the Ex-Date will already include the effect of prior splits if any. This is why the Split Ratio is included only in news releases and never displayed on screens.

