In order to make a decision, you need to consider several factors. Here are some of them:
Costs associated with selling or renting the property;
Your financial needs and expectations for income;The time needed for the transaction (sale) or for finding and moving into a new tenant (rent);
Local real estate market trends and demands;Personal preferences and preferences related to home ownership versus rental.
You can calculate how much you may get from the sale based on the current market price and relevant fees. At the same time, determine the rental cost for your property using similar properties in the area as a reference. You can also consult realtors or local property managers to get more accurate data. In the end, analyze all this information and decide which option is better for you in light of your personal circumstances.

The IRS does not release specific information about what factors may increase your chances of an audit. However, the agency has stated that various factors can affect your risk for an examination, including:
Your income level and tax returns;
How you file your taxes (e.g., whether you itemize or take the standard deduction);
Whether you claim certain types of deductions or credits; and If you have a history of filing amended returns or being selected for audit in the past.
Generally speaking, taxpayers with higher incomes, complex returns, or those who make more aggressive claims on their tax forms are more likely to be auditioned than others. It's also worth noting that audits are conducted through a random selection process, so even if you fit one or more of these risk factors, you may still not be chosen for review. If you believe you need to discuss your tax situation with someone, consider contacting a tax professional.
